.TH smi-decrypt "1" "June 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Encryption Command"
.SH NAME
smi-decrypt \- Decrypt and verify a file encrypted with smi-encrypt
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-decrypt
.I ENCRYPTED_FILE
.RI [ OUTPUT ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B smi-decrypt
decrypts the specified
.I ENCRYPTED_FILE
(usually with a
.B .enc
suffix) using OpenSSL with AES-256-CBC symmetric decryption.
The passphrase is read interactively from the terminal without echo.
.PP
Before decrypting,
.B smi-decrypt
verifies the HMAC-SHA-512 integrity tag stored in
.IR ENCRYPTED_FILE .hmac .
If the tag does not match, decryption is aborted and exit code
.B 3
is returned.
This detects both accidental corruption and intentional tampering.
.PP
Key derivation uses PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA-256 and 600\,000 iterations with the salt stored
in the encrypted file header, matching the parameters used by
.BR smi-encrypt (1).
.PP
The command is suitable for decrypting both
.B text and binary files
of any size.
There is
.B no upper limit on file size
imposed by the key or block size.
.PP
The passphrase is entered via the terminal (stdin) without any graphical or TUI pinentry dialog,
making the command safe to use over SSH and in non-graphical environments.
.PP
Without
.IR OUTPUT ,
the decrypted file is written next to
.I ENCRYPTED_FILE
without the
.B .enc
suffix. With
.IR OUTPUT ,
it is written to that file, and
.B \-
writes the decrypted content to standard output instead of a file.
.I ENCRYPTED_FILE
and its
.I .hmac
file are kept unchanged, so the file can be decrypted again later.
.PP
An existing decrypted file, the default one or
.IR OUTPUT ,
is never overwritten: it may be from an earlier run and changed since.
When it exists,
.B smi-decrypt
stops with exit code
.B 1
before asking for the passphrase. Remove or rename it to decrypt again.
This also protects a file without the
.B .enc
suffix, whose decrypted name would be its own name.
.PP
.B Note:
files encrypted with earlier versions of this tool (10\,000 iterations, no HMAC file) are not
compatible with the current version and must be decrypted with the matching older version.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I ENCRYPTED_FILE
Path to the AES-256-CBC encrypted file to decrypt, typically produced by
.BR smi-encrypt (1).
Must be an existing regular file.
A corresponding
.I ENCRYPTED_FILE.hmac
integrity file must also be present.
Both files are kept.
.TP
.I OUTPUT
Optional. Where the decrypted content goes:
.RS
.TP
.B \-
standard output. No file is written. The passphrase prompt and the error messages go to standard error, so
standard output carries the decrypted content only. Nothing is written when the integrity check fails; when the
decryption itself fails, part of the content may have been written already.
.TP
empty or not given
.I ENCRYPTED_FILE
without the
.B .enc
suffix.
.TP
any other value
the name of the file to write. It must not exist.
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
File decrypted successfully; encrypted file and HMAC file kept.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid usage: wrong number of arguments, encrypted file does not exist, HMAC file not found,
or the decrypted file exists already.
.TP
.B 2
Decryption failed.
.TP
.B 3
Integrity check failed: the HMAC does not match.
The file may be corrupted or tampered with.
No files are modified.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Decrypt a previously encrypted configuration file:
.IP
smi-decrypt secrets.conf.enc
.PP
The decrypted output is written to
.IR secrets.conf ;
.I secrets.conf.enc
and
.I secrets.conf.enc.hmac
stay.
.PP
Decrypt to another file:
.IP
smi-decrypt secrets.conf.enc /tmp/secrets.conf
.PP
Show the content without writing a file:
.IP
smi-decrypt secrets.conf.enc \- | less
Encrypt a file first with:
.IP
smi-encrypt secrets.conf
.SH AUTHOR
.PP
Command written by Imre Tabur.
.SH "REPORTING BUGS"
.PP
Report smi-decrypt bugs to \fIinfo@setmy.info\fR
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
.BR smi-encrypt (1),
.BR openssl (1)
